MAC Alias Cache

OPNsense resolves MAC-type firewall aliases through a cache that keeps
the last addresses of a MAC which host discovery no longer reports for
up to 12 hours. This plugin shows what each MAC alias resolves to and
rebuilds every MAC alias, and every alias nesting one, from current
host discovery data on demand (Firewall > Diagnostics > MAC Alias Cache,
or "configctl macaliascache flush").

Plugin Changelog
================

1.3

* Both configd actions name allowed_groups (wheel, wwwonly): a local
  account in neither group can neither flush nor read the MAC and
  address list through status
* A flush reads core's reply. The page warns when core reports an error
  loading a table, and the reply carries core's status and messages
* A table core's update left empty is flushed only after an update that
  ran to its end and answered. After one that crashed, was killed or
  printed no reply, such a table keeps its addresses, and the page says
  so
* A MAC alias whose lifetime in core's alias table file is not above 0
  (an update frequency that comes to less than a second) can never be
  rebuilt by a flush; status and the page name it. The lifetime is read
  as core reads it, fractions included
* The page shows the texts of core and of the plugin as written, without
  HTML entities

1.2_1

* The page shows core's fix for core #10114 as an information note, since
  it may make the plugin unnecessary, and no longer shows core's fix for
  core #10950, which leaves nothing to do; status lists both

1.2

* The core-contract check also covers the host list's columns and flag
  that core's cache reads, and the expiry a flush relies on; a flush
  refuses when core changed any of them
* Core's fix for core #10950 shows as an information note instead of a
  warning, and the plugin now also notes core's fix for core #10114;
  neither blocks a flush
* The host list's source shows as a line on the page; host discovery
  being off is no longer a warning
* The page compares cached and current addresses in canonical form

1.1_2

* The source moved to github.com/LegoTypes/plugins; no change to
  the plugin.

1.1_1

* A flush keeps core's MAC alias cache at core's own mode (0640) instead of
  making it world-readable, and narrows one an earlier flush widened

1.1

* State and lock files move out of /tmp into a root-owned directory; files are
  checked for type, owner and shape when read back
* Flush refuses read-only users and runs one at a time
* Host rows are validated (MAC and IP shape) before they reach the alias cache;
  the core files the plugin relies on are checked, and a flush refuses to run
  when they no longer match

1.0

* Initial release
